course: dictionary
lesson: 10935
title: Vulgar: rude or just cheap-looking?
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one the writer means. Look at the words around the tricky word first. «Vulgar» often means rude and crude, especially about the body or the toilet. Those are the clues to look for.
explain: Sometimes vulgar means lacking good taste: showy and cheap-looking. Imagine a room covered in gold glitter and fake jewels. It looks flashy, but not elegant. Ask yourself: is the sentence about rude jokes, or about things that look cheap and too much?
explain: In the story, the Mouse cries, «Would you like cats if you were me?» and calls cats «nasty, low, vulgar things». Here vulgar means common and rude. Try the swap test: put rude in place of vulgar and check that the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Vulgar means rude and crude, especially about the body or the toilet.
fact: Vulgar also means lacking good taste; showy and cheap-looking.
fact: A vulgar fraction is an old UK maths name for a common fraction, like 3/4 or 2/5.
fact: In «nasty, low, vulgar things», vulgar means common and rude.
fact: Some words have several meanings, so always check the context before you decide.
exercise: The Mouse calls cats «nasty, low, vulgar things». What does vulgar mean here? | *Common and rude | Kind and gentle | Quiet and shy | Very tall and thin
exercise: The hotel lobby had gold taps, fake diamonds and loud carpets. Mum says it looks vulgar. What does she mean? | Very old and historic | Clean and tidy | Empty and quiet | *Showy and cheap-looking
exercise: In an old UK maths book, what is a fraction like 3/4 called? | A percentage | A whole number | *A vulgar fraction | A decimal
test: Ruby says vulgar always means rude swearing. What is the best reply? | It means very old | It means giving out light | Yes, it always means rude swearing | *Not always: it can also mean showy and cheap-looking
test: Which example shows vulgar meaning lacking good taste? | A vulgar fraction like 2/5 | A vulgar joke told at the dinner table | *A vulgar gold chandelier covered in fake stones | A vulgar bark from a noisy dog
exam: Mia reads about «vulgar jokes» in one book and a «vulgar fraction» in an old maths text. What do the two uses mean? | Funny, and a whole number | Very old, and a long fraction | *Rude and crude, and a common fraction | Showy and expensive, and a decimal
